        272.24566650390625,
        260.885986328125,
        281.9983825683594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "g(δ(i), θ) ≤0, i = 1, . . . , N",
      "bbox": [
        270.84796142578125,
        269.60675048828125,
        392.05767822265625,
        281.7583923339844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "θ ∈Rnθ.",
      "bbox": [
        270.84796142578125,
        286.144775390625,
        310.3736572265625,
        298.29638671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(7)",
      "bbox": [
        509.2799377441406,
        269.013671875,
        521.9999389648438,
        278.7663879394531
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The solution to (7) is the minimum-volume set that contains sample points δ(1), . . . , δ(N), and guar-",
      "bbox": [
        89.99993896484375,
        310.2917785644531,
        522.0048828125,
        322.13739013671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "antees P{V (R(θ)) > ϵ} ≤β. The algorithms we present in this section solve (7) given arbitrary",
      "bbox": [
        89.99990844726562,
        325.1492004394531,
        521.9955444335938,
        335.6873779296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "values of ϵ, β ∈(0, 1) and N samples.",
      "bbox": [
        89.9998779296875,
        338.6982116699219,
        257.5052490234375,
        349.23638916015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "3.1. Tiling with Basis Functions",
      "bbox": [
        89.99986267089844,
        359.8787536621094,
        238.6254425048828,
        369.68603515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We first present a method to construct a sublevel set function g(x, θ) that is convex in θ but noncon-",
      "bbox": [
        89.99986267089844,
        378.2931213378906,
        521.99755859375,
        388.22039794921875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "vex in x, as was done in (Devonport, 2023). While convex scenario optimization methods can be",
      "bbox": [
        89.99984741210938,
        391.8421325683594,
        521.997314453125,
        401.7694091796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "used to analyze this approach, they require large sample sizes and are not computationally efficient.",
      "bbox": [
        89.99984741210938,
        405.5657043457031,
        522.0,
        415.31842041015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We show that by utilizing the nonconvex scenario optimization tools introduced in Section 2, we",
      "bbox": [
        89.99984741210938,
        419.1156921386719,
        521.9999389648438,
        428.868408203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "can significantly improve upon these limitations. To construct g(x, θ), select a finite set of basis",
      "bbox": [
        89.99984741210938,
        432.4901428222656,
        522.0046997070312,
        442.41741943359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "functions f1(x), ..., fm(x) : RD →R and take g to be",
      "bbox": [
        89.99984741210938,
        441.46923828125,
        326.2935791015625,
        456.7922668457031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "g(x, θ) =",
      "bbox": [
        258.6738586425781,
        476.525146484375,
        300.781494140625,
        486.2124328613281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "m",
      "bbox": [
        307.9398498535156,
        464.9288330078125,
        315.4317321777344,
        472.00628662109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "X",
      "bbox": [
        303.80584716796875,
        473.2967224121094,
        319.55859375,
        480.278564453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "i=1",
      "bbox": [
        304.8328552246094,
        491.65185546875,
        318.5312805175781,
        498.72930908203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "θifi(x)",
      "bbox": [
        321.3818359375,
        476.5251770019531,
        353.3275146484375,
        487.279296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(8)",
      "bbox": [
        509.27984619140625,
        477.4367370605469,
        521.9998779296875,
        487.189453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In this section, we will use this approach to construct a tiling of the state space and estimate the",
      "bbox": [
        106.93585205078125,
        507.94573974609375,
        522.0012817382812,
        517.698486328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "reachable set of a given problem. To create this tiling, assume that the reachable set, R, is contained",
      "bbox": [
        89.99981689453125,
        520.7092895507812,
        522.0009765625,
        531.2474975585938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "in a subset A ⊆RD. We then partition A into m cells, creating a collection of sets A1, ..., Am such",
      "bbox": [
        89.99981689453125,
        532.9498291015625,
        522.0001831054688,
        546.4324951171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "that ∪m",
      "bbox": [
        89.99981689453125,
        546.4988403320312,
        124.12871551513672,
        558.3455200195312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "i=1Ai = A and Ai ∩Aj = ∅∀i. This approach can produce arbitrarily fine estimates of the",
      "bbox": [
        116.6368179321289,
        547.8073120117188,
        521.9981079101562,
        560.59130859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "reachable set, depending on how refined the partition is. The accuracy of the partition increases as",
      "bbox": [
        89.99983215332031,
        562.1417236328125,
        521.9998779296875,
        571.8944702148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "m increases. Further, we define 1Ai to be the zero-one indicator function for the set Ai, so that",
      "bbox": [
        89.99983215332031,
        575.5172119140625,
        521.994873046875,
        588.3474731445312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "1Ai(x) = 1 if x ∈Ai and 1Ai(x) = 0 otherwise. Therefore, the reachable set estimate is a union",
      "bbox": [
        89.99984741210938,
        588.4552612304688,
        522.0038452148438,
        600.7008056640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of the partitioned cells. We write this as a constrained optimization problem:",
      "bbox": [
        89.99986267089844,
        602.7897338867188,
        424.2872619628906,
        612.54248046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "minimize",
      "bbox": [
        199.2108612060547,
        632.9107666015625,
        240.42543029785156,
        642.6635131835938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "θ",
      "bbox": [
        217.724853515625,
        642.2538452148438,
        221.6780242919922,
        649.331298828125
      ],
      "direction": [
